Subject: [PATCH 0/7] media: i2c: ov7251: fixes and infrared strobe for Surface Book 2

The Microsoft Surface Book 2 has an OV7251 infrared camera (used by
Windows Hello) next to its front camera, both on the Intel IPU3 (image
processing unit) camera interface. Its 850 nm emitter is wired to the
sensor's STROBE output pin: the ACPI tables describe no LED, GPIO line
or regulator for it. With the mainline driver the camera streams, but
always unlit.

Patches 1 to 5 fix issues found while testing the driver on this
machine:

- 1: the link frequency index is taken from the firmware list instead
  of the driver's table, so the sensor runs at 240 MHz while the
  firmware asks for 319.2 MHz (Fixes, Cc stable).
- 2: Daniel Scally's patch switching the gain control to
  V4L2_CID_ANALOGUE_GAIN, carried by linux-surface.
- 3: control events, the one v4l2-compliance failure on the sensor.
- 4: the 60 and 90 fps frame intervals are accepted but the sensor
  keeps streaming at 30 fps, because the vertical blanking control is
  not updated and rewrites the 30 fps frame length at stream start.
- 5: orientation and rotation controls from firmware.

Patches 6 and 7 add the emitter. 6 moves the strobe registers, which
the three mode tables write identically, out of the tables without
functional change. 7 adds a V4L2_CID_FLASH_STROBE_OE control that
enables the STROBE pin, pulses it on every frame and makes the pulse
follow the exposure, from 52 lines up to 45% of the frame. The Windows
driver on this hardware clamps it to 52 to 776 lines, 45% of the 30 fps
frame.
Filmed with a phone at locked exposure, the emitter is 1.1x (default
exposure) to 1.2x (776-line pulse) as bright as during a Windows Hello
sign-in on the same unit. Windows reportedly strobes alternate frames;
a 0xaa pattern is a one-line change if preferred.

Tested on a Surface Book 2 (i7-8650U) with the series built out of
tree against linux-surface 7.2.12:

- link frequency 319200000, pixel rate 63840000, 30.01 fps unchanged
- v4l2-compliance -u on the sensor: 0 failures (was 1)
- --set-subdev-fps 60 / 90: 60.15 / 90.45 fps measured (was 30.01)
- orientation front, rotation 0
- dark frames and the sensor test pattern unchanged after patch 6
- strobe span read back while streaming: 504 lines at exposure 504,
  776 at exposure 1704 (30 fps), 258 at exposure 552 (90 fps)
- mean pixel value of an indoor infrared frame at analogue gain 128:
  20 unlit, 624 with the strobe enabled at exposure 504, 706 at 1704
- strobe off and sensor suspended within 2 s of the stream stopping
- 10 unbind/unload/load cycles: no warning in the kernel log

The remaining v4l2-compliance failures are in the ipu3-cio2 capture
driver (buffer creation and mapping checks) and unchanged by this
series.

Antoine Pichot (6):
  media: i2c: ov7251: Fix link frequency index
  media: i2c: ov7251: Add control event support
  media: i2c: ov7251: Reset vblank when the frame interval changes
  media: i2c: ov7251: Add orientation and rotation controls
  media: i2c: ov7251: Move the strobe registers out of the mode tables
  media: i2c: ov7251: Add strobe output enable control

Daniel Scally (1):
  media: i2c: Clarify that gain is Analogue gain in OV7251

